SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Ryan Ng (University of Melbourne) – Abu
 ndance of ideas\, R&D subsidies\, and patent policy
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3064
DESCRIPTION:Ryan Ng: “Abundance of ideas\, R&D subsidies\, and patent pol
 icy”\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/81347271549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWX
 iaW65kUhzrKAvS.1\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8
 \n\nABSTRACT:\nThis paper examines the interplay between the patent system
  and R\\&D subsidies\, investigating how the optimal mix is shaped by scar
 city of ideas. We consider an environment of innovation that distinguishes
  between exploratory stage and development stage. The innovator begins wit
 h an exogenously given idea whose quality is unknown\, and which is not ye
 t patentable. By undertaking costly exploratory research\, the innovator p
 rivately observes the idea’s quality and converts it into a patentable i
 nvention. In the development stage\, the innovator decides whether to furt
 her incur development cost to transform the invention into a marketable in
 novation and realize the returns from her idea. High-quality ideas satisfy
  patentability criteria whereas low-quality do not and generate social cos
 ts if granted protection. The social planner simultaneously chooses how mu
 ch to subsidize idea exploration process and how lenient the patent office
  should be. Subsidizing promotes idea exploration and permits stricter pat
 ent screening\, but it also imposes social costs. We show that when high-q
 uality ideas are abundant\, subsidies alone optimally induce idea explorat
 ion. But when they are scarce\, it is socially optimal to combine subsidie
 s with lenient patent screening regime that occasionally protects low-qual
 ity ideas.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/81347271549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWXiaW65kUhzrKAv
 S.1
END:VEVENT
